.\" SPDX-License-Identifier: BSD-2-Clause
.Dd October 10, 2026
.Dt BSD_CACHE 3
.Os
.Sh NAME
.Nm bsd_cache_open ,
.Nm bsd_cache_lookup ,
.Nm bsd_cache_generation ,
.Nm bsd_cache_put_if_current ,
.Nm bsd_cache_create ,
.Nm bsd_cache_put ,
.Nm bsd_cache_get ,
.Nm bsd_cache_remove ,
.Nm bsd_cache_purge ,
.Nm bsd_cache_set_limits ,
.Nm bsd_cache_set_pressure ,
.Nm bsd_cache_get_stats ,
.Nm bsd_cache_destroy
.Nd bounded application caches with automatic pressure eviction
.Sh LIBRARY
.Li libcache
.Pq Fl lcache
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.In bsd_cache.h
.Ft int
.Fn bsd_cache_open "size_t entries" "size_t cost" "struct bsd_cache **out"
.Ft "enum bsd_cache_result"
.Fn bsd_cache_lookup "struct bsd_cache *cache" "const char *key" "void *value" "size_t *length"
.Ft int
.Fn bsd_cache_create "size_t entries" "size_t cost" "unsigned flags" "bsd_cache_evicted_fn callback" "void *argument" "struct bsd_cache **out"
.Ft int
.Fn bsd_cache_put "struct bsd_cache *cache" "const char *key" "const void *value" "size_t length" "size_t cost"
.Ft int
.Fn bsd_cache_get "struct bsd_cache *cache" "const char *key" "void *value" "size_t *length"
.Ft int
.Fn bsd_cache_remove "struct bsd_cache *cache" "const char *key"
.Ft int
.Fn bsd_cache_purge "struct bsd_cache *cache"
.Ft int
.Fn bsd_cache_set_limits "struct bsd_cache *cache" "size_t entries" "size_t cost"
.Ft int
.Fn bsd_cache_set_pressure "struct bsd_cache *cache" "unsigned level"
.Ft int
.Fn bsd_cache_get_stats "struct bsd_cache *cache" "struct bsd_cache_stats *out"
.Ft int
.Fn bsd_cache_destroy "struct bsd_cache *cache"
.Ft int
.Fn bsd_cache_generation "struct bsd_cache *cache" "uint64_t *generation"
.Ft int
.Fn bsd_cache_put_if_current "struct bsd_cache *cache" "const char *key" "const void *value" "size_t length" "size_t cost" "uint64_t generation"
.Sh DESCRIPTION
For the simple interface, call
.Fn bsd_cache_open
with entry and byte-cost limits.
It enables automatic pressure handling with no application notification loop
or eviction callback required.
Use
.Fn bsd_cache_create
for explicit flags and callbacks.
.Pp
.Fn bsd_cache_lookup
returns
.Dv BSD_CACHE_HIT
with a copied value,
.Dv BSD_CACHE_MISS
when the entry is absent, or
.Dv BSD_CACHE_ERROR
with
.Va errno
set for an actual error.
A miss leaves the buffer and length unchanged.
Hits and misses preserve
.Va errno .
An undersized buffer is an error, not a miss, and reports the required length.
The lookup performs the presence check and copy under one lock.
Do not perform a separate validity check before reading an entry.
The returned copy remains usable even if pressure immediately evicts the entry.
.Pp
When rebuilding data outside the cache lock, capture a token with
.Fn bsd_cache_generation
before reading the source, then insert with
.Fn bsd_cache_put_if_current .
The token check and insertion happen under one lock.
An intervening invalidation causes
.Er ESTALE
and leaves existing entries untouched.
Tokens belong to one cache instance and must not be reused after destruction.
Any successful insertion, purge, remove attempt (including a missing key),
limit change, or change of effective pressure invalidates prior tokens.
The generation is cache-wide: unrelated writes may conservatively reject
an insertion, which callers can skip or retry by rebuilding from the source.
A token never wraps: exhaustion returns
.Er EOVERFLOW
from the getter and refuses conditional insertion.
Plain put intentionally inserts without this check.
.Pp
Cache presence does not establish freshness against changing application data.
Use versioned keys, remove affected keys, or purge the cache when the source
changes; memory-pressure notifications do not detect those source changes.
Notifications are asynchronous; critical pressure empties the cache when its
watcher processes the notification, not at the instant system pressure rises.
Warning pressure trims the budget without necessarily emptying the cache.
.Pp
The cache owns copies of keys and byte values, with explicit entry and cost
limits and least-recently-used eviction.
Entries are disposable: callers must handle cache misses and reconstruct data.
Keys are nonempty strings of at most 255 bytes; values are at most 16 MiB.
At most 65536 entries are permitted.
Cost must be at least the value length; callers may charge a larger logical
cost.
Metadata and key storage are additionally bounded by the entry limit.
A balanced-tree key index bounds lookup comparisons logarithmically in the
number of entries, including sorted or common-prefix keys.
Its links are stored within each entry; it does not allocate a separate bucket
array or require allocation during lookup.
The LRU list still determines eviction order.
Index, LRU order, accounting and copied lookup values are protected by the
same cache mutex.
Changing limits immediately evicts entries exceeding the effective budget.
Replacing a key copies the new value before changing the old entry.
Insertion checks pressure, budget and any generation token before allocating
the copy, then rechecks under the lock before changing the cache.
Pressure arriving during the copy may still cause that allocation to be
discarded.
.Pp
Get copies data into caller-owned storage and updates LRU order.
The length argument supplies capacity and receives the required or copied size.
Insufficient capacity returns
.Er ENOMEM
without a partial copy; missing keys return
.Er ENOENT .
Other failures leave outputs unchanged.
No pointer into an evictable entry escapes the cache.
.Pp
With
.Dv BSD_CACHE_AUTOMATIC ,
a private thread watches BSDNotify's reserved
.Va system.memory.pressure
state, reconnecting after failures.
No privileged publisher attribute is needed to observe it.
Warning pressure halves both budgets; critical pressure purges the cache and
rejects insertions with
.Er EAGAIN
until recovery.
Normal pressure restores configured limits.
Missing or unavailable state is reported as unknown and preserves the last
known restriction; initial unknown state uses configured limits.
The cache remains bounded when the notification service is absent.
Failed client opens, including
.Er EAGAIN ,
are reported in the notification error statistic and retried at most once per
second.
An ordinary unchanged-state watch timeout does not report a notification error.
The first notification snapshot, a changed watcher epoch, or skipped state
generations purge entries and invalidate rebuild tokens: a coalesced critical
and recovery transition must not leave older cached entries behind.
The manual pressure setter applies the same policy to this cache only.
.Pp
Statistics include current entries and cost, configured limits, hits, misses,
limit/pressure evictions, current pressure, and the last notification error.
The optional callback receives key, value, length, reason, and argument after
an entry is detached and the cache lock is released.
Its key and value are borrowed only for the callback duration.
Callbacks may inspect or operate on the cache, but must not destroy it.
Insertions during destruction callbacks are refused with
.Er EAGAIN .
They can run on the calling thread or the automatic watcher thread and must
synchronize their own application state.
.Pp
Operations are synchronized across threads.
Destroy requires exclusive ownership after all application calls and callbacks
have finished, stops the watcher, joins it, then frees remaining entries.
Each watcher iteration uses one one-second libservice RPC budget across
client discovery, HELLO negotiation, reconnect and the state watch.
An outstanding notification RPC can delay destruction until that budget expires.
The budget covers RPC waits, not scheduling delays, mutex acquisition, arbitrary
kernel work or application callbacks.
Destruction does not cancel application callbacks.
A watcher callback attempting destruction is refused with
.Er EDEADLK .
Inherited cache objects cannot be used or destroyed in a child after fork;
create a fresh cache there.
.Sh RETURN VALUES
Except for the three-result lookup described above, functions return zero
on success or minus one with
.Va errno
set.
Invalid inputs return
.Er EINVAL ;
inherited handles return
.Er ECHILD .
A value exceeding the current effective cost budget returns
.Er EAGAIN .
Allocation and thread errors are also returned.
.Sh EXAMPLES
.Bd -literal -offset indent
struct bsd_cache *cache;
char value[256];
size_t length = sizeof(value);
if (bsd_cache_open(128, 8 * 1024 * 1024, &cache) == -1)
        return (-1);
switch (bsd_cache_lookup(cache, "thumbnail-v3", value, &length)) {
case BSD_CACHE_HIT:
        /* Use value: this copy survives later cache eviction. */
        break;
case BSD_CACHE_MISS:
        /* Rebuild value from its source, then optionally cache it.
         * Failure to cache must not prevent using rebuilt data. */
        break;
case BSD_CACHE_ERROR:
        /* Handle errno; ENOMEM means the output buffer is too small. */
        break;
}
/* Finish application users before destruction. */
bsd_cache_destroy(cache);
.Ed
.Pp
For a rebuild that must not repopulate an invalidated cache:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
uint64_t generation;
if (bsd_cache_generation(cache, &generation) == 0) {
        /* Read the source and build bytes after capturing the token. */
        if (bsd_cache_put_if_current(cache, key, bytes, size, size,
            generation) == -1) {
                /* ESTALE: discard this cache insertion.
                 * EAGAIN: pressure or budget prevents caching.
                 * Handle other errors as appropriate. */
        }
}
.Ed
A returned value is a snapshot; the library cannot guarantee that an external
source stays unchanged after lookup or reconstruction.
Applications needing stronger consistency must coordinate source updates
with their own synchronization and cache invalidation.
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr notify_state_watch 3 ,
.Xr BSDMemory 8 ,
.Xr notifyctl 8
